  "image_name": "1d61de31f97ca513e06ff9431b013597.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how matrix-remodeling enzymes around neurons and blood vessels change over time in two contrasting situations: synaptic strengthening and later neurovascular injury. In the top row, long-term potentiation is shown over minutes to hours. A neuronal network with dendrites and surrounding extracellular matrix appears to undergo a brief, localized increase in protease activity near synapses, opening or loosening the perisynaptic matrix. This remodeling is then followed by a more regulated phase in which activity becomes restricted and later shut down, suggesting transient matrix cleavage that supports plasticity without prolonged damage.\n\nThe lower row shifts to the brain microenvironment around a capillary, with endothelial cells, pericytes, astrocytes, and microglia. Initially the vessel wall and surrounding matrix appear intact. Over hours to days and then days to weeks, multiple matrix metalloproteinases spread through the tissue and around the vasculature. The figure suggests breakdown of vascular and extracellular matrix barriers, movement of inflammatory cells or factors into the tissue, and increasing disruption around neurons. In the final stage, neurons are labeled as dying or dead, indicating that sustained protease activity is associated with neuroinflammation, blood–brain barrier compromise, and neuronal loss.",
